<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  X4MP on-foot presence, MD side (M3b). Lua side: ui/x4mp_onfoot.lua; native side: features/onfoot
  (docs/research/on-foot-presence.md 3.1-3.3, ADR-046).

  Why MD at all: native can read the player's parent-relative pose and container, but not which ROOM the player stands in -
  the SDK has no room accessor (sdk/x4n_rooms.h is only an enum -> name mapper). So MD builds the room key and pushes it over the
  bridge:

    MD -> Lua  raise_lua_event "x4mp.onfoot_room" "<mode=..;sector=..;smacro=..;sx=..;sy=..;sz=..;rmacro=..;ax=..;ay=..;az=..;kind=..>"
    Lua -> native x4mp.onfoot_room {"v":1,"data":"<the same string>"}   (ui/x4mp_onfoot.lua)

  Field notes (the parser is features/onfoot/onfoot_types.cpp):
    mode    0 = not on foot in a station interior, 1 = walking, 2 = seated in a cockpit (the ship is the container)
    sector, smacro, sx/sy/sz   the station's sector macro, its macro and its sector-space position in metres. MD has no round(), so
            the 50 m bucket (the cross-client match key of ADR-009) is computed natively from these numbers.
    rmacro  the room's macro id;  ax/ay/az  the room origin in the container (relativeposition), metres with decimals - native
            rounds to decimetres, which S10.2 showed to be stable across runs and reloads (raw anchors drift by <= 0.5 mm).
    kind    1 = static room, 2 = dynamic interior.
  roomtype is NOT sent: MD prints $R.type as a name, not as the X4RoomType ubyte, so the native default (21 = none) stands.

  Triggers: every room change of the player entity (immediate, so the peers see the avatar appear in the new room without waiting for
  the poll), plus a 1 Hz pull from ui/x4mp_onfoot.lua that produces the first report after a load and the leave state (mode=0) when
  the player steps out. Both only signal one report cue, so the report is written once.

  Remote avatars (W4). The same mdscript also owns the temporary crew NPCs that stand in for the other players:

    native -> Lua  x4mp.onfoot_actors  {"v":1,"spawn":[{pid,name,race,macro,rmacro,ax,ay,az,kind,x,y,z,yaw},...],"despawn":[pid,...]}
    Lua -> MD      AddUITriggeredEvent("X4MP_OnFoot", "spawn", {pid,name,race,macro,rmacro,ax,ay,az,kind,x,y,z,yaw})
                   AddUITriggeredEvent("X4MP_OnFoot", "despawn", {pid,...})
    MD -> Lua      raise_lua_event "x4mp.onfoot_actor" "spawn;<pid>;<actor>" | "gone;<pid>"
    MD -> native   nothing: the position and the look come from the network every frame, native writes them with
                   SetPositionalOffset (S10.4: p95 0.163 m at 32 commands/s). This file only creates, places once and
                   destroys the actor; it never moves it afterwards, so there is no per-frame MD cost.

  Why the actor is a cue actor of cue="namespace" with customhandler: it must not run an NPC brain of its own (it would walk
  away from the network position) and it must not be written into the save as an NPC. temporary stays false - the actor is
  removed by X4MP_OnFoot_Despawn or by the janitor, which is also what makes a stale avatar disappear after a reload.

  Teardown (S10.6). When a zone loses attention the engine despawns the container's dynamic interiors and the avatars standing in
  them die with the rooms; the list and the native side would keep pointing at dead ids. X4MP_OnFoot_InteriorsDespawn listens to
  event_object_interiors_despawning (vanilla cleans up its own temporary characters the same way, md/npc_instantiation.xml
  PlaceNPC_InteriorDespawning), destroys what is still alive, drops every entry, tells native "gone" per player and logs the
  leftover count: "[X4MP] onfoot interiors despawn: cleared 2 avatar(s), leftover=0".

  Safety: the only components this file creates are those crew NPCs (faction.x4mp_team_1, tag.crew, the requested race or the
  character macro a peer picked); the only things it destroys are actors listed in md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors; and it never moves the
  player or any vanilla object.
-->
<mdscript name="X4MP_OnFoot" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="md.xsd">
  <cues>

    <!-- the 1 Hz pull from Lua -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Tick" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'report'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <signal_cue_instantly cue="X4MP_OnFoot_Report"/>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- the player changed room: event.param is the new room, event.param2 the previous one (S10.1) -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_RoomChanged" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_object_changed_room object="player.entity"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <signal_cue_instantly cue="X4MP_OnFoot_Report"/>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- one report. Always raises the event, even when the answer is "not on foot", so the peers can drop the avatar. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Report" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_cue_signalled/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <set_value name="$Report" exact="'mode=0'"/>
        <set_value name="$Mode" exact="1"/>
        <set_value name="$Cont" exact="player.container"/>
        <set_value name="$Room" exact="player.room"/>
        <do_if value="player.occupiedship">
          <!-- in a pilot seat: the ship is the container. The report still carries the fields: a bare "mode=2" is rejected by the
               native parser (it needs the container to know which ship), which used to log "bad room report (field sector)" once a
               second. Without a resolvable container the seated state is reported as the leave state instead. -->
          <set_value name="$Mode" exact="2"/>
        </do_if>
        <!-- A player on foot inside a station must report where he stands, and every part of that key can be missing: a dynamic interior
             does not always expose relativeposition, and player.container can be null for a frame after a lift or a save load. Each
             failure is logged (filter general) instead of silently reporting mode=0 - that silent path is what made two players in the
             same office invisible to each other. -->
        <do_if value="$Cont and $Room">
          <do_if value="$Cont.macro.id and $Room.macro.id">
            <set_value name="$Pos" exact="$Cont.position"/>
            <do_if value="$Pos and $Cont.sector">
              <set_value name="$Anchor" exact="@$Room.relativeposition.{$Cont}"/>
              <do_if value="not $Anchor">
                <!-- Fallback: both objects still have a sector-space position, so the room's offset inside the container is their
                     difference. (A dynamic interior that hides relativeposition still sits at a real position.) -->
                <set_value name="$Anchor" exact="[$Room.position.x - $Pos.x, $Room.position.y - $Pos.y, $Room.position.z - $Pos.z]"/>
              </do_if>
              <do_if value="$Anchor">
                <set_value name="$Report" exact="'mode=' + $Mode + ';sector=' + $Cont.sector.macro.id + ';smacro=' + $Cont.macro.id + ';sx=' + ($Pos.x / 1m) + ';sy=' + ($Pos.y / 1m) + ';sz=' + ($Pos.z / 1m) + ';rmacro=' + $Room.macro.id + ';ax=' + ($Anchor.x / 1m) + ';ay=' + ($Anchor.y / 1m) + ';az=' + ($Anchor.z / 1m) + ';kind=' + (if $Room.dynamicinterior then 2 else 1)"/>
              </do_if>
              <do_else>
                <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot report: no anchor room=' + $Room.macro.id + ' cont=' + $Cont.macro.id" filter="general" context="false"/>
              </do_else>
            </do_if>
            <do_else>
              <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot report: no position/sector cont=' + $Cont.macro.id" filter="general" context="false"/>
            </do_else>
          </do_if>
          <do_else>
            <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot report: no macro id cont-room'" filter="general" context="false"/>
          </do_else>
        </do_if>
        <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_room'" param="$Report"/>
        <!-- M3c: keep the room catalogue of this container current. Native needs the macro id strings of all rooms, because the
             wire carries only a hash and MD cannot hash; without them an avatar in another room of the same station cannot be
             placed. Pushed when the container changes AND once every 10 reports: a native DLL reload empties the table while the
             container stays the same, and only a periodic push recovers from that (Guest ran with rooms=0 after a reload).
             The tick counts unconditionally and the identity is a STRING: an object held in a global does not survive a save load
             intact, which is why the comparison silently stopped firing (host: rooms=0 for minutes after a load). -->
        <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsTick" exact="(md.$X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsTick? or 0) + 1"/>
        <set_value name="$ContKey" exact="(if $Cont and $Cont.macro.id then $Cont.macro.id else '')"/>
        <do_if value="$ContKey != '' and (not md.$X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsKey? or md.$X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsKey != $ContKey or md.$X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsTick ge 10)">
          <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsKey" exact="$ContKey"/>
          <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsTick" exact="0"/>
          <signal_cue_instantly cue="X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsBuild"/>
        </do_if>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- the room macro ids of the current container, one per room (M3c). Also asked once per UI session, because a DLL reload
         loses the table on the native side while the container has not changed. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Rooms" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'rooms'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <signal_cue_instantly cue="X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsBuild"/>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_RoomsBuild" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_cue_signalled/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <set_value name="$Ids" exact="''"/>
        <!-- The local room macro always goes in first: it is the one the OTHER client has to resolve when this player walks into
             another room, and find_room does not list a dynamic interior, so a thin catalogue would otherwise leave the avatar
             unplaceable. Everything find_room returns is added on top (station first - the spike measured the full list there -
             then the container, in case the two objects differ). -->
        <do_if value="player.room and player.room.macro.id">
          <set_value name="$Ids" exact="$Ids + player.room.macro.id + ';'"/>
        </do_if>
        <set_value name="$St" exact="player.station"/>
        <set_value name="$Co" exact="player.container"/>
        <do_if value="$St and $St.macro.id">
          <find_room name="$FromStation" object="$St" multiple="true"/>
        </do_if>
        <do_if value="$Co and $Co.macro.id">
          <find_room name="$FromContainer" object="$Co" multiple="true"/>
        </do_if>
        <do_for_each name="$Rm" in="$FromStation">
          <do_if value="$Rm.macro.id">
            <set_value name="$Ids" exact="$Ids + $Rm.macro.id + ';'"/>
          </do_if>
        </do_for_each>
        <do_for_each name="$Rm" in="$FromContainer">
          <do_if value="$Rm.macro.id">
            <set_value name="$Ids" exact="$Ids + $Rm.macro.id + ';'"/>
          </do_if>
        </do_for_each>
        <do_if value="not @$FromStation.count and not @$FromContainer.count">
          <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot rooms: find_room returned nothing for station/container, local room only'" filter="general" context="false"/>
        </do_if>
        <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_rooms'" param="$Ids"/>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- ===================== remote avatars (W4) ===================== -->

    <!-- Create one avatar. event.param3 is the Lua table, read positionally: 1 pid, 2 name, 3 race, 4 character macro (empty =
         let MD pick a crew NPC), 5 room macro, 6/7/8 room anchor in decimetres, 9 kind, 10/11/12 ROOM-local position in
         metres at FOOT height (native sends the actor's parent-relative pose, which is eye height, and removed the 1.72 m), 13 yaw in degrees.
         The position is ROOM-local: add_actor_to_room treats <position> as an offset inside $Room (the spike's of_place proved it, and
         vanilla moves the player this way). The room is looked up by macro id plus anchor because one room macro can appear several
         times in a container; M3c adds the nearest-copy fallback when the exact copy is not here.
         Native re-asks every 2 s until it gets an answer, so a failure here is retried rather than lost. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Spawn" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'spawn'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <set_value name="$Pid" exact="event.param3.{1}"/>
        <set_value name="$Name" exact="event.param3.{2}"/>
        <set_value name="$RaceName" exact="event.param3.{3}"/>
        <set_value name="$Macro" exact="event.param3.{4}"/>
        <set_value name="$RMacro" exact="event.param3.{5}"/>
        <set_value name="$Ax" exact="event.param3.{6}"/>
        <set_value name="$Ay" exact="event.param3.{7}"/>
        <set_value name="$Az" exact="event.param3.{8}"/>
        <set_value name="$X" exact="event.param3.{10} * 1m"/>
        <set_value name="$Y" exact="event.param3.{11} * 1m"/>
        <set_value name="$Z" exact="event.param3.{12} * 1m"/>
        <set_value name="$Yaw" exact="event.param3.{13} * 1deg"/>
        <do_if value="not md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors?">
          <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors" exact="[]"/>
        </do_if>
        <set_value name="$Room" exact="null"/>
        <set_value name="$Fallback" exact="null"/>
        <set_value name="$BestD" exact="100000000"/>
        <set_value name="$Hits" exact="0"/>
        <set_value name="$Anchors" exact="0"/>
        <set_value name="$Cont" exact="player.container"/>
        <!-- Diagnostics only: the failure text below must not itself error on a null container or room. -->
        <set_value name="$ContMacro" exact="'-'"/>
        <do_if value="$Cont">
          <set_value name="$ContMacro" exact="$Cont.macro.id"/>
        </do_if>
        <set_value name="$MyMacro" exact="'-'"/>
        <do_if value="player.room">
          <set_value name="$MyMacro" exact="player.room.macro.id"/>
        </do_if>
        <do_if value="$Cont and $Cont.macro.id">
          <!-- The player's own room first (the common case: two players in the same room). find_room does not list the dynamic
               interior the player stands in on this build ("no room room_gen_managersoffice_01_macro", 05.11.2026), but
               player.room is that very room, and the wire only names it when the peer's room hash equals ours. -->
          <do_if value="player.room and player.room.macro.id == $RMacro">
            <!-- The native names a room by macro only when the peer's room key equals the viewer's own (its hash covers the
                 sector, the station and the anchor), so in the same-room case player.room IS the room. No anchor check: the
                 key already proved it, and relativeposition is not exposed for every dynamic interior. -->
            <set_value name="$Room" exact="player.room"/>
          </do_if>
          <!-- Any other room of the container: match the macro id, then the anchor, so several copies of one room macro are told apart. -->
          <do_if value="not $Room">
            <find_room name="$Rooms" object="$Cont" multiple="true"/>
            <do_for_each name="$Rm" in="$Rooms">
              <do_if value="not $Room and $Rm.macro.id == $RMacro">
                <set_value name="$Hits" exact="$Hits + 1"/>
                <set_value name="$Anchor" exact="@$Rm.relativeposition.{$Cont}"/>
                <!-- relativeposition is not exposed for every dynamic interior - the same reason the report needs a fallback. The
                     room's sector position minus the container's is where the room's origin sits inside the container. -->
                <do_if value="not $Anchor and $Rm.position and $Cont.position">
                  <set_value name="$Anchor" exact="[$Rm.position.x - $Cont.position.x, $Rm.position.y - $Cont.position.y, $Rm.position.z - $Cont.position.z]"/>
                </do_if>
                <set_value name="$D2" exact="1000000"/>
                <do_if value="$Anchor">
                  <set_value name="$Anchors" exact="$Anchors + 1"/>
                  <set_value name="$Dx" exact="($Anchor.x / 1m) * 10 - $Ax"/>
                  <set_value name="$Dy" exact="($Anchor.y / 1m) * 10 - $Ay"/>
                  <set_value name="$Dz" exact="($Anchor.z / 1m) * 10 - $Az"/>
                  <set_value name="$D2" exact="$Dx * $Dx + $Dy * $Dy + $Dz * $Dz"/>
                  <do_if value="$D2 le 25">
                    <set_value name="$Room" exact="$Rm"/>
                  </do_if>
                </do_if>
                <!-- Nearest copy of this macro, and the FIRST one unconditionally: an anchor match must never be the difference
                     between an avatar and nothing. The host log showed the case this guards - hits=1 anchors=1 and a "no room"
                     line, i.e. a room with the right macro whose anchor did not compare (05.11.2026). -->
                <do_if value="not $Room">
                  <do_if value="not $Fallback">
                    <set_value name="$Fallback" exact="$Rm"/>
                    <set_value name="$BestD" exact="$D2"/>
                  </do_if>
                  <do_elseif value="$D2 lt $BestD">
                    <set_value name="$BestD" exact="$D2"/>
                    <set_value name="$Fallback" exact="$Rm"/>
                  </do_elseif>
                </do_if>
              </do_if>
            </do_for_each>
          </do_if>
        </do_if>
        <!-- M3c, fallback F2: the exact copy of the room is not in this container, so the remote player stands in another copy of
             the same room macro. Show them in the nearest copy instead of showing nothing; the position still comes from the
             network every frame. -->
        <do_if value="not $Room and $Fallback">
          <set_value name="$Room" exact="$Fallback"/>
          <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot spawn: no exact room ' + $RMacro + ' at ' + $Ax + '/' + $Ay + '/' + $Az + ' (hits=' + $Hits + ' anchors=' + $Anchors + ' d2=' + $BestD + '), using the nearest copy for ' + $Name" filter="general" context="false"/>
        </do_if>
        <!-- A race string cannot be passed to <select>, only a race object can (vanilla always passes one: gmc_madscientist
             race="$Manager.race"). So the wire's "argon" ... is mapped here; an unknown or empty race falls back to the
             local player's race. -->
        <set_value name="$Race" exact="player.entity.race"/>
        <do_if value="$RaceName == 'argon'">
          <set_value name="$Race" exact="race.argon"/>
        </do_if>
        <do_elseif value="$RaceName == 'boron'">
          <set_value name="$Race" exact="race.boron"/>
        </do_elseif>
        <do_elseif value="$RaceName == 'paranid'">
          <set_value name="$Race" exact="race.paranid"/>
        </do_elseif>
        <do_elseif value="$RaceName == 'split'">
          <set_value name="$Race" exact="race.split"/>
        </do_elseif>
        <do_elseif value="$RaceName == 'teladi'">
          <set_value name="$Race" exact="race.teladi"/>
        </do_elseif>
        <do_elseif value="$RaceName == 'terran'">
          <set_value name="$Race" exact="race.terran"/>
        </do_elseif>
        <do_if value="$Room">
          <set_faction_active faction="faction.x4mp_team_1" active="true"/>
          <set_value name="$A" exact="null"/>
          <!-- Re-parenting instead of recreating: a room change used to destroy and create the actor, which changed its face (the
               catalogue's generic character macros are randomised per creation) and flashed the model on every door. If this pid
               already has an avatar, that same component moves into the new room below. -->
          <do_if value="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors?">
            <do_for_each name="$C" in="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors">
              <do_if value="not $A and @$C.$x4mp_pid == $Pid">
                <set_value name="$A" exact="$C"/>
              </do_if>
            </do_for_each>
          </do_if>
          <set_value name="$New" exact="not $A"/>
          <!-- Diagnostic for the "the other player's character changes on every load" report: an empty macro means a random crew
               NPC, so a line here says whether the spawn raced the appearance. Only a new avatar needs a look. -->
          <do_if value="$New and (not $Macro or $Macro == '')">
            <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot spawn: no character macro for pid=' + $Pid + ' - a random crew NPC is used'" filter="general" context="false"/>
          </do_if>
          <do_if value="$New">
            <!-- A chosen look is a character macro: <create_cue_actor macro="..."> is how vanilla spawns a named character
                 (md/scenario_tutorials.xml:18520). Without one, a crew NPC is picked by race (S10.7: <select> honours
                 neither race nor gender, so it is the fallback and not the picker). -->
            <do_if value="$Macro and $Macro != ''">
              <create_cue_actor name="$A" cue="namespace" macro="$Macro">
                <name name="$Name"/>
                <owner exact="faction.x4mp_team_1"/>
              </create_cue_actor>
            </do_if>
            <do_else>
              <create_cue_actor name="$A" cue="namespace">
                <select tags="tag.crew" race="$Race"/>
                <name name="$Name"/>
                <owner exact="faction.x4mp_team_1"/>
              </create_cue_actor>
            </do_else>
          </do_if>
          <!-- The same avatar changing room: leave the old room first (the engine keeps an actor in the room it was placed in),
               then enter the new one below with its new pose. destroy="false" keeps the component, hence the face. -->
          <do_if value="$A and $A.$x4mp_room and $A.$x4mp_room != $Room">
            <remove_actor_from_room actor="$A" destroy="false"/>
          </do_if>
          <do_if value="$A">
            <set_entity_traits entity="$A" customhandler="true" temporary="false"/>
            <set_entity_overrides entity="$A" title="$Name"/>
            <do_if value="$New">
              <set_value name="$A.$x4mp_pid" exact="$Pid"/>
              <set_value name="$A.$x4mp_name" exact="$Name"/>
            </do_if>
            <!-- Remembered per actor: the walk cue needs the room it was created in, and an avatar can sit in another room of the
                 same station (M3c), where player.room would be the wrong one. -->
            <set_value name="$A.$x4mp_room" exact="$Room"/>
            <add_actor_to_room actor="$A" object="$Room" result="$Moved">
              <position x="$X" y="$Y" z="$Z"/>
              <rotation yaw="$Yaw"/>
            </add_actor_to_room>
            <do_if value="$Moved">
              <!-- The container is remembered so the interiors teardown can recognise its own event.object even when an actor's
                   own .object is already gone. -->
              <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Cont" exact="$Cont"/>
              <do_if value="$New"><append_to_list name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors" exact="$A"/></do_if>
              <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_actor'" param="'spawn;' + $Pid + ';' + $A"/>
            </do_if>
            <do_else>
              <!-- a new actor is not in the list, so nothing would ever despawn it: destroy it here and let native retry.
                   An existing avatar is kept in its previous room; the next request may succeed. -->
              <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot spawn: add_actor_to_room failed for ' + $Name" filter="general"/>
              <do_if value="$New">
                <destroy_object object="$A"/>
              </do_if>
            </do_else>
          </do_if>
          <do_else>
            <!-- the build does not have the macro the peer picked: nothing to show, native retries in 2 s -->
            <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot spawn: no character ' + $Macro + ' for the avatar of ' + $Name" filter="general"/>
          </do_else>
        </do_if>
        <do_else>
          <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot spawn: no room ' + $RMacro + ' in the player container for ' + $Name + ' (cont=' + $ContMacro + ' myroom=' + $MyMacro + ' hits=' + $Hits + ' anchors=' + $Anchors + ')'" filter="general" context="false"/>
        </do_else>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- The walk hybrid. The engine exposes no animation call to native, so an actor that is moved by SetPositionalOffset every frame
         stands in the idle pose (S10.4 mode D: the walk animation dies the moment the pose is overwritten). MD's start_actor_walk is
         the only way into the locomotion state, so while the sender walks native raises this cue every 400 ms and stops writing the
         pose for 200 ms around it; the leg motion comes from the engine's own walk. event.param3: 1 pid, 2 speed (m/s).
         The target must be a SLOT (there is no walk-to-position form): the nearest free standing slot to the actor is used, because
         the walk only has to keep the locomotion state alive for the hold window and a close slot keeps the actor near the true
         path. Slots already occupied by another of our avatars are skipped - two actors on one spot look wrong. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Walk" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'walk'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <set_value name="$Pid" exact="event.param3.{1}"/>
        <set_value name="$Speed" exact="event.param3.{2}"/>
        <set_value name="$A" exact="null"/>
        <do_if value="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors?">
          <do_for_each name="$C" in="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors">
            <do_if value="not $A and @$C.$x4mp_pid == $Pid">
              <set_value name="$A" exact="$C"/>
            </do_if>
          </do_for_each>
        </do_if>
        <!-- Native re-issues this every 400 ms for as long as the sender walks. If the engine is already walking this actor, a new
             start_actor_walk would restart the leg - that is the visible jerk - and the slot scan below is MD work per command for
             nothing. Finish the current leg; native keeps the pose off until the walk stops. -->
        <do_if value="$A and not @$A.iswalking">
          <!-- The room the avatar was created in (set by the spawn cue), not player.room: M3c places avatars in other rooms too. -->
          <set_value name="$Room" exact="@$A.$x4mp_room"/>
          <do_if value="not $Room">
            <set_value name="$Room" exact="player.room"/>
          </do_if>
          <do_if value="$Room">
            <find_npc_slot name="$Slots" object="$Room" tags="tag.stand" excludeblocked="true" excludefilled="true" multiple="true"/>
            <set_value name="$SlotsFrom" exact="'tag.stand'"/>
            <do_if value="not @$Slots.count">
              <find_npc_slot name="$Slots" object="$Room" tags="tag.npc_generic" excludeblocked="true" excludefilled="true" multiple="true"/>
              <set_value name="$SlotsFrom" exact="'tag.npc_generic'"/>
            </do_if>
            <do_if value="not @$Slots.count">
              <find_npc_slot name="$Slots" object="$Room" anytag="[tag.service, tag.wait]" excludeblocked="true" excludefilled="true" multiple="true"/>
              <set_value name="$SlotsFrom" exact="'work_slot_fallback'"/>
            </do_if>
            <do_if value="not @$Slots.count">
              <find_npc_slot name="$Slots" object="$Room" excludeblocked="false" excludefilled="false" multiple="true"/>
              <set_value name="$SlotsFrom" exact="'any_slot_including_filled'"/>
            </do_if>
            <set_value name="$Slot" exact="null"/>
            <set_value name="$SlotIdx" exact="0"/>
            <set_value name="$BestD" exact="-1m"/>
            <set_value name="$Near" exact="null"/>
            <set_value name="$NearD" exact="-1m"/>
            <do_if value="@$Slots.count">
              <do_all exact="$Slots.count" counter="$i">
                <set_value name="$Taken" exact="false"/>
                <do_for_each name="$C" in="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors">
                  <do_if value="not $Taken and @$C != $A and (@$C.distanceto.{$Slots.{$i}}) lt 1.5m">
                    <set_value name="$Taken" exact="true"/>
                  </do_if>
                </do_for_each>
                <do_if value="not $Taken">
                  <!-- A slot has no .position; distance is always measured from an entity to a slot. -->
                  <set_value name="$D" exact="@$A.distanceto.{$Slots.{$i}}"/>
                  <do_if value="$NearD lt 0m">
                    <set_value name="$Near" exact="$Slots.{$i}"/>
                    <set_value name="$NearD" exact="$D"/>
                  </do_if>
                  <do_else>
                    <do_if value="$D lt $NearD">
                      <set_value name="$Near" exact="$Slots.{$i}"/>
                      <set_value name="$NearD" exact="$D"/>
                    </do_if>
                  </do_else>
                  <!-- Nearest slot at least 1 m away: a slot under the actor's feet finishes the walk immediately and shows no animation. -->
                  <do_if value="$D ge 1m">
                    <do_if value="$BestD lt 0m or $D lt $BestD">
                      <set_value name="$Slot" exact="$Slots.{$i}"/>
                      <set_value name="$SlotIdx" exact="$i"/>
                      <set_value name="$BestD" exact="$D"/>
                    </do_if>
                  </do_if>
                </do_if>
              </do_all>
            </do_if>
            <do_if value="not $Slot and $Near">
              <set_value name="$Slot" exact="$Near"/>
              <set_value name="$BestD" exact="$NearD"/>
            </do_if>
            <do_if value="$Slot">
              <start_actor_walk actor="$A" target="$Slot" speed="$Speed"/>
              <do_if value="$SlotsFrom != 'tag.stand'">
                <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot walk: pid=' + $Pid + ' stage=' + $SlotsFrom + ' slots=' + @$Slots.count + ' idx=' + $SlotIdx + ' d=' + ($BestD / 1m) + ' speed=' + $Speed" filter="general" context="false"/>
              </do_if>
            </do_if>
            <do_else>
              <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot walk: no free slot for pid=' + $Pid + ' stage=' + $SlotsFrom + ' slots=' + @$Slots.count + ' room=' + (@$Room.type)" filter="general" context="false"/>
              <!-- Tell native to stop holding the pose: there is nothing to animate here, and a withheld pose would freeze the
                   avatar until the sender stops walking. -->
              <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_actor'" param="'failed;' + $Pid"/>
            </do_else>
          </do_if>
          <do_else>
            <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot walk: no room for pid=' + $Pid" filter="general" context="false"/>
            <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_actor'" param="'failed;' + $Pid"/>
          </do_else>
        </do_if>
        <do_else>
          <do_if value="not $A">
            <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot walk: no actor for pid=' + $Pid" filter="general" context="false"/>
          </do_if>
          <!-- else: the actor exists and is already walking; the leg is left to finish (see the guard above). -->
        </do_else>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- The sender stopped walking: end the slot walk, native resumes exact placement on the next frame and snaps the actor back. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_StopWalk" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'stop_walk'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <set_value name="$Pid" exact="event.param3.{1}"/>
        <set_value name="$A" exact="null"/>
        <do_if value="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors?">
          <do_for_each name="$C" in="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors">
            <do_if value="not $A and @$C.$x4mp_pid == $Pid">
              <set_value name="$A" exact="$C"/>
            </do_if>
          </do_for_each>
        </do_if>
        <do_if value="$A">
          <stop_actor_walk actor="$A"/>
        </do_if>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- Remove the avatars of the players native no longer wants shown: event.param3 is a list of player ids. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Despawn" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'despawn'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <do_if value="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors?">
          <do_for_each name="$Pid" in="event.param3">
            <do_all exact="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.count" counter="$i" reverse="true">
              <do_if value="@md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}.$x4mp_pid == $Pid">
                <destroy_object object="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}"/>
                <remove_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}"/>
              </do_if>
            </do_all>
            <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_actor'" param="'gone;' + $Pid"/>
          </do_for_each>
        </do_if>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- 5 s after every load, driven by ui/x4mp_onfoot.lua (a <delay>, event_game_loaded and check_interval are all silent
         no-ops in this mdscript - S10 spike). Removes the avatars a crashed or killed session left in the save. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Janitor" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'janitor'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <do_if value="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors?">
          <do_all exact="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.count" counter="$i" reverse="true">
            <set_value name="$Pid" exact="@md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}.$x4mp_pid"/>
            <destroy_object object="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}"/>
            <remove_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}"/>
            <!-- Tell native the actor is gone, or it keeps writing a pose to a dead id and never asks for a new one. -->
            <do_if value="$Pid">
              <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_actor'" param="'gone;' + $Pid"/>
            </do_if>
          </do_all>
        </do_if>
        <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors" exact="[]"/>
        <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Cont" exact="null"/>
        <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot janitor: avatars cleared'" filter="general"/>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- S10.6: the container's dynamic interiors are about to be despawned (the zone lost attention, or the player flew away). The
         engine takes the avatars with the rooms, so what is left here are dead ids: destroy the ones that still live, drop every
         entry and tell native 'gone', or it keeps writing poses to ids that no longer exist and never asks for a new one. The
         leftover count in the log line is what the S10.6 check reads ("0 leftover actors"). -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_InteriorsDespawn" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <!-- The event requires an object attribute (without it the game logs "Required attribute 'object' is missing" and passes a
             null event.object). Our stored container is the only one that can own avatars, so listen to exactly that one. -->
        <event_object_interiors_despawning object="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Cont"/>
        <check_value value="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors? and md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.count gt 0"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <set_value name="$Object" exact="event.object"/>
        <set_value name="$Cleared" exact="0"/>
        <!-- Our own container: event.object is exactly what the spawn cue stored, so every avatar we own is in it. -->
        <set_value name="$All" exact="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Cont? and event.object == md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Cont"/>
        <do_all exact="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.count" counter="$i" reverse="true">
          <set_value name="$A" exact="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}"/>
          <set_value name="$Drop" exact="not @$A.exists"/>
          <do_if value="not $Drop and ($All or @$A.object == $Object)">
            <set_value name="$Drop" exact="true"/>
          </do_if>
          <do_if value="$Drop">
            <set_value name="$Pid" exact="@$A.$x4mp_pid"/>
            <do_if value="@$A.exists">
              <destroy_object object="$A"/>
            </do_if>
            <remove_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}"/>
            <do_if value="$Pid">
              <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_actor'" param="'gone;' + $Pid"/>
            </do_if>
            <set_value name="$Cleared" exact="$Cleared + 1"/>
          </do_if>
        </do_all>
        <do_if value="$All">
          <set_value name="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Cont" exact="null"/>
        </do_if>
        <!-- Only our container is worth a line: every zone in the game despawns interiors now and then. -->
        <do_if value="$All or $Cleared gt 0">
          <debug_text text="'[X4MP] onfoot interiors despawn: cleared ' + $Cleared + ' avatar(s), leftover=' + md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.count" filter="general"/>
        </do_if>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- ======================================================= the Talk menu (W5) ======================================================= -->
    <!--
      S10.7 proved the shape: a conversation with an actor that carries customhandler="true" starts the vanilla "default" conversation,
      our cue adds choices, and a pick arrives as event_conversation_next_section with event.param = the section and event.param2 = the
      choiceparam. The actor is event.object. "Message" is not a wire action - the vanilla chat window cannot be prefilled, so the Lua
      side prints the /w command; "Wave" goes to native, is relayed to the target only, and every viewer plays it on that actor.
      The choices only appear on our avatars: the $x4mp_pid variable is what tells them apart from a vanilla NPC.

      M5b adds the two economy actions to the same menu. Neither is a new wire action: both open the economy screen with
      the other player already picked as the recipient, and the screen's own buttons (send this amount, propose a trade)
      go through the economy verb the /mp commands use. That keeps one code path and one audit trail per action.
    -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Talk" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_conversation_started conversation="default"/>
        <check_value value="event.object.$x4mp_pid"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <add_player_choice text="{92000,940}" section="x4mp_mp_wave" choiceparam="'wave'" position="top_left"/>
        <add_player_choice text="{92000,941}" section="x4mp_mp_message" choiceparam="'message'" position="left"/>
        <add_player_choice text="{92000,943}" section="x4mp_mp_credits" choiceparam="'credits'" position="right"/>
        <add_player_choice text="{92000,944}" section="x4mp_mp_trade" choiceparam="'trade'" position="bottom_left"/>
        <add_player_choice text="{92000,945}" section="x4mp_mp_invite" choiceparam="'invite'" position="bottom"/>
        <add_player_choice_return text="{92000,942}" position="bottom_right"/>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_TalkChoice" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_conversation_next_section sectionprefix="x4mp_mp_"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <raise_lua_event name="'x4mp.onfoot_talk'"
                         param="event.param2 + ';' + event.object.$x4mp_pid + ';' + event.object.$x4mp_name"/>
      </actions>
    </cue>

    <!-- A wave from another player: play it on that player's actor. set_actor_emotion is the action vanilla uses for the emote
         vocabulary (npc_state_machines.xml: nod01, gesticulate01, crossarms01, ...); a temporary actor has no state machine of its own,
         so the emote is a one-shot and the next SetPositionalOffset frame leaves the pose alone. -->
    <cue name="X4MP_OnFoot_Wave" instantiate="true">
      <conditions>
        <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4MP_OnFoot'" control="'wave'"/>
      </conditions>
      <actions>
        <do_if value="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors?">
          <do_for_each name="$Pid" in="event.param3">
            <do_all exact="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.count" counter="$i" reverse="true">
              <do_if value="@md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}.$x4mp_pid == $Pid">
                <set_actor_emotion actor="md.$X4MP_OnFoot_Actors.{$i}" emotion="'nod01'"/>
              </do_if>
            </do_all>
          </do_for_each>
        </do_if>
      </actions>
    </cue>

  </cues>
</mdscript>
